A viable native Track B family has emerged from Proposition 4.2 and the development rule in Proposition 4.1: recover a fixed-length slice of one ESWC codeword by locating a point in consecutive developed \(B_{(t,j)}\) columns. Exhaustive row scanning costs \(O(Lq)\), while the cyclic-development invariant reduces the slice to one initial value plus \(L-1\) modular recurrences; the verifier can check every row exactly. I’m validating this against the local harness and the answer/operation caps before committing to it.
